rivate prison system in the u.s  if they actually make money per inmate  they  are more incentivized to have more  inmates  so issues like uh public security  um should not be privatized so in this  clip we see police forces that  actually look much more like the police  force we're used to so they're in  uniform  normal clothing they there's very little  riot gear here  or there's less of it than in the  previous clip that we've seen and i  think this is  because the game actually wants to be  closer to our reality it wants to be  more like the world that we know giving  you an authentic feeling of this is how  things could go with the technologies  that we already have  and give you more of a creepy feeling  when these people become exploitative  i find it interesting in this clip that  they've chosen to name the company  albion albion is an old antique name for  the british isles it's interesting to go  back to that reflection of these good  old times when  law and order was provided by knights so  there's this poetic moment to it  naming that albion next let's take a  look at weapons  cyberware cyberpunk 2077  there's a aggressively masculine  tendency in cyberpunk and i think this  actually goes with the idea of these  visceral fight scenes they're using  swords they're using the  mantis blade which of course doesn't  make much sense if you can actually have  railguns  why would you even get close enough to  be using blades but i think it actually  in a game it looks much better  and it's nicer gameplay choosing to use  a weapon that is  implanted has of course the very usual  advantages  in the sense that you can hide the  weapon better it is actually seamlessly  integrated into your arm  so when you're under cover when you're  doing stealth work you could actually  move through recognizable scanners and  so on and just look like you have a  cyber arm  you can now actually you know shoot  someone just by pointing a finger this  is a power fantasy is the idea i